{
    "Domain-1" : {
      "overview" : "This domain assesses an organization's ultimate control over its data, independent of external jurisdictions or political influences. It goes beyond basic data residency by focusing on legal control, access, and encryption management. Maturity here confirms that data location is actively governed by the organization's legal and business requirements, rather than dictated solely by a cloud provider or foreign law. Achieving high maturity ensures the organization can unilaterally secure, access, and move its data while remaining compliant with domestic regulations, particularly regarding cross-border transfers and governmental data access requests.",
      "qnum" : "1",
      "title" : "Data Sovereignty",
      "1": "Data Residency & Location",
      "1-summary": "Defines and enforces where data is physically stored based on legal and business requirements.",
      "1-tier": "Foundation",
      "1-points": "1",
      "1-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Draft a Data Residency Policy mandating that all new critical data is stored in approved sovereign regions.  </p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Configure cloud storage services (e.g., S3, Azure Blob) to use specific local regions only.</p>",
      "2": "Data Protection & Privacy",
      "2-summary": "Basic compliance with common data protection and privacy regulations.",
      "2-tier": "Foundation",
      "2-points": "2",
      "2-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Conduct a Data Privacy Impact Assessment (DPIA) against baseline local regulations.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Implement Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) across all data repositories.</p>",
      "3": "Data Classification and Inventory",
      "3-summary": "Systematic identification and tagging of all data assets by sensitivity and compliance needs.",
      "3-tier": "Foundation",
      "3-points": "3",
      "3-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Establish a simple, mandatory data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential).</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Utilize Discovery Tools to scan data stores and automatically tag sensitive data.</p>",
      "4": "Legal & Jurisdictional Control",
      "4-summary": "Ability to resist extra-territorial data requests and maintain control under home jurisdiction.",
      "4-tier": "Strategic",
      "4-points": "4",
      "4-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Review and renegotiate vendor contracts to specify local law and jurisdiction for data access requests.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Implement 'Zero Trust' policies for all non-local user and service access attempts.</p>",
      "5": "Cryptographic Key Management Control",
      "5-summary": "Ownership and exclusive control over all encryption keys for sensitive data, independent of vendors.",
      "5-tier": "Strategic",
      "5-points": "6",
      "5-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Mandate that all root encryption keys are generated and stored in a physically controlled location/HSM.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Implement a Customer-Managed Key (CMK) solution using a dedicated Hardware Security Module (HSM).</p>",
      "6": "Workload Data Protection & Privacy",
      "6-summary": "Mechanisms to protect data during processing within critical applications and workloads.",
      "6-tier": "Strategic",
      "6-points": "5",
      "6-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Define a policy requiring encryption of data in use (e.g., Confidential Computing) for defined workloads.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Deploy Homomorphic Encryption or Confidential Computing services for highly sensitive data processing.</p>",
      "7": "Data Flow and Transfer Auditing",
      "7-summary": "Real-time monitoring and immutable logging of all data flows, transfers, and access events.",
      "7-tier": "Advanced",
      "7-points": "7",
      "7-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Establish a dedicated Data Sovereignty Audit Team to review all cross-border data transfers monthly.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Deploy a Data Loss Prevention (DLP) tool configured to log all egress traffic to non-sovereign destinations.</p>",
      "8": "Data Access by Third Parties Policies",
      "8-summary": "Strict, audited, and revocable control over any third-party access to organisational data.",
      "8-tier": "Advanced",
      "8-points": "8",
      "8-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Establish a quarterly review process for all third-party and vendor data access logs.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Integrate Single Sign-On (SSO) and time-limited access tokens for all external parties.</p>"
      },
    "Domain-2" : {
      "overview" : "Technical Sovereignty evaluates the degree of control an organization maintains over the foundational components of its technology stack—from the underlying hardware and firmware to the application source code and runtime environments. High maturity in this domain signifies a deliberate reduction in reliance on proprietary interfaces and single-vendor ecosystems. The organization actively manages vendor lock-in risk by prioritizing portability and open standards, ensuring they possess the ability to rebuild or migrate their critical functions onto a different technology platform if necessary, mitigating risks posed by geopolitical shifts or vendor failures.",
      "qnum" : "2",
      "title" : "Technical Sovereignty",
      "1": "Technology Stack Ownership & Control",
      "1-summary": "Basic knowledge and documentation of the technology components currently in use.",
      "1-tier": "Foundation",
      "1-points": "1",
      "1-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Create a detailed inventory of all commercial and open-source software licenses and dependencies.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Map the Top 10 mission-critical services to their underlying technology and ownership.</p>",
      "2": "Vendor Lock-in Risk Mitigation",
      "2-summary": "Basic analysis of proprietary dependencies and initial efforts to diversify technology providers.",
      "2-tier": "Foundation",
      "2-points": "2",
      "2-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Identify the Top 5 vendors where migration would be financially or technically prohibitive.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Conduct a 'break glass' exercise to simulate losing access to a core vendor service.</p>",
      "3": "Standardised Technical Framework Adoption",
      "3-summary": "Use of widely accepted, non-proprietary industry standards for core technical components.",
      "3-tier": "Foundation",
      "3-points": "3",
      "3-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Adopt a policy prioritizing technologies based on global open standards (e.g., Kubernetes).</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Migrate a non-critical application to a containerized environment using an open source orchestrator.</p>",
      "4": "Interoperability and Portability Strategy",
      "4-summary": "A defined plan and architecture enabling easy migration between different technical environments.",
      "4-tier": "Strategic",
      "4-points": "4",
      "4-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Document a 3-year portability roadmap detailing how core services could be migrated to an alternative platform.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Implement cloud-agnostic tools for data/compute orchestration (e.g., Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management, Ansible).</p>",
      "5": "Hardware and Infrastructure Source Verification",
      "5-summary": "Verification and control over the origin and integrity of core computing hardware and firmware.",
      "5-tier": "Strategic",
      "5-points": "5",
      "5-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Require and review supply chain provenance documentation (Bill of Materials) for all new hardware purchases.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Use Trusted Platform Module (TPM) chips and Secure Boot to verify hardware integrity at startup.</p>",
      "6": "Self-Hosted Application Runtime Control",
      "6-summary": "Hosting and control of application execution environments (runtime) on owned or controlled platforms.",
      "6-tier": "Strategic",
      "6-points": "6",
      "6-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Define a policy requiring critical core applications to run on infrastructure entirely controlled by the organization.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Deploy and manage a private internal container runtime environment (e.g., on-premises OpenShift).</p>",
      "7": "Code and Intellectual Property Control",
      "7-summary": "Full control, ownership, and independent hosting of all critical application source code and IP.",
      "7-tier": "Advanced",
      "7-points": "7",
      "7-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Establish a legal policy asserting explicit IP ownership over all custom software developed, internal or external.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Host all primary source code repositories (e.g., Git) on an organization-controlled server in a sovereign location.</p>",
      "8": "Future-Proofing Technology Roadmaps",
      "8-summary": "Strategic planning to ensure technological choices are resilient to geopolitical or vendor shifts.",
      "8-tier": "Advanced",
      "8-points": "8",
      "8-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Integrate a Geopolitical Risk Assessment phase into the standard technology procurement lifecycle.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Invest in emerging open standards that offer superior control and reduce reliance on proprietary interfaces.</p>"
      },
    "Domain-3" : {
      "overview" : "This domain examines the organization's autonomy and independence in executing critical business and IT operations. It ensures that essential functions can be performed without reliance on external human expertise or infrastructure outside of the organization's direct control or trusted sovereign borders. Key areas include internal skill development, resilience planning (e.g., Disaster Recovery that withstands geopolitical isolation), and transparent supply chain vetting. High maturity confirms that the organization has robust, locally managed processes and personnel ready to maintain operations and execute incident response free from external interference.",
      "qnum" : "3",
      "title" : "Operational Sovereignty",
      "1": "Operational Process Documentation",
      "1-summary": "Documented procedures for routine and critical IT operations.",
      "1-tier": "Foundation",
      "1-points": "1",
      "1-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Create Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) for core maintenance and provisioning tasks.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Use a knowledge base system (e.g., Wiki, Confluence) to centralize all operational documentation.</p>",
      "2": "Dependency on External Managed Services",
      "2-summary": "Identification of key external operational dependencies (e.g., outsourced functions and functions).",
      "2-tier": "Foundation",
      "2-points": "2",
      "2-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Compile a register of all outsourced operational functions (e.g., NOC, monitoring, patching).</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Automate a simple, non-critical operational task using an in-house automation engine.</p>",
      "3": "Access Control and Identity Management",
      "3-summary": "Basic policies and systems to manage user access to resources.",
      "3-tier": "Foundation",
      "3-points": "3",
      "3-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Enforce Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) for all administrative and remote access accounts.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Implement a centralized Identity Provider (IdP) for all employee and vendor access.</p>",
      "4": "Internal Skills and Competency Development",
      "4-summary": "Active programs to build and retain in-house expertise for critical sovereign technologies.",
      "4-tier": "Strategic",
      "4-points": "4",
      "4-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Launch a specialized training program to upskill internal staff on the architecture of critical sovereign systems.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Shadow external support personnel to document knowledge transfer for critical procedures.</p>",
      "5": "Disaster Recovery and Business Continuity",
      "5-summary": "Full, tested ability to recover and maintain operations without relying on foreign infrastructure.",
      "5-tier": "Strategic",
      "5-points": "5",
      "5-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Design and annually test a sovereign DR plan that assumes all external global connectivity is severed.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Geographically disperse backup copies of critical data within sovereign zones.</p>",
      "6": "Supply Chain Transparency and Vetting",
      "6-summary": "Formal program to vet and monitor the security and geopolitical risk of all key technology suppliers.",
      "6-tier": "Strategic",
      "6-points": "6",
      "6-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Include Sovereignty Clauses in contracts with Tier 1 suppliers, demanding deeper transparency.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Utilize Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) tools to track components in third-party software.</p>",
      "7": "Sovereign Incident Response Plan",
      "7-summary": "A dedicated and tested incident response plan that limits external interference during crises.",
      "7-tier": "Advanced",
      "7-points": "7",
      "7-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Develop a parallel Incident Response Team trained only to use sovereign-controlled tools and communications.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Establish a dedicated 'clean room' network segment for incident analysis using certified local hardware.</p>",
      "8": "Operational Autonomy in Critical Functions",
      "8-summary": "Ability to perform all critical business and technical operations entirely within controlled boundaries.",
      "8-tier": "Advanced",
      "8-points": "8",
      "8-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Identify all functions that must remain operational under complete geopolitical isolation.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Deploy and maintain dark fiber or equivalent sovereign network paths for critical functions.</p>"
    },
    "Domain-4" : {
      "overview" : "Assurance Sovereignty addresses the right, capability, and transparency required to verify the security and compliance claims of both internal systems and external vendors. It's the mechanism by which trust is verified, not assumed. This domain covers control over all audit and security monitoring data, ensuring logs are retained locally for independent analysis. Advanced maturity means the organization has contractual rights to mandate independent, sovereign-led inspections of provider environments and utilizes continuous technical validation to ensure that security controls remain effective against defined sovereign risks.",
      "qnum" : "4",
      "title" : "Assurance Sovereignty",
      "1": "Regular Security Audits Conducted",
      "1-summary": "Periodic internal or external assessments of security controls.",
      "1-tier": "Foundation",
      "1-points": "1",
      "1-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Schedule and execute annual penetration tests and vulnerability scans on core systems.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Implement a Vulnerability Management System to track and prioritize remediation efforts.</p>",
      "2": "Control over Security Monitoring Data",
      "2-summary": "Logging and storage of basic security events and alerts.",
      "2-tier": "Foundation",
      "2-points": "2",
      "2-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Define a policy mandating local storage of all security logs for a minimum retention period.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Deploy a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system within the organization's network perimeter.</p>",
      "3": "Risk Management Framework",
      "3-summary": "A basic framework for identifying and assessing geopolitical and sovereignty-related risks.",
      "3-tier": "Foundation",
      "3-points": "3",
      "3-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Integrate a basic geopolitical risk filter into the existing Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) process.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Identify and document the 'Top 5' geopolitical risks that could impact IT operations.</p>",
      "4": "Compliance with Local Security Standards",
      "4-summary": "Certification against national or local industry-specific security and sovereign standards.",
      "4-tier": "Strategic",
      "4-points": "4",
      "4-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Achieve formal certification (e.g., ISO 27001 or equivalent local standard) for critical infrastructure.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Utilize a Cloud Compliance Tool to continuously monitor configurations against required sovereign baselines.</p>",
      "5": "Transparency in Vendor Security Practices",
      "5-summary": "Contractual obligation for vendors to provide detailed, auditable reports on their security posture.",
      "5-tier": "Strategic",
      "5-points": "5",
      "5-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Require a right-to-audit clause in all contracts for providers hosting sensitive data.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Use third-party vendor risk platforms to review and score supplier security posture.</p>",
      "6": "Independent Certification and Vetting",
      "6-summary": "The right and ability to mandate independent third-party audits of all critical vendors/platforms.",
      "6-tier": "Advanced",
      "6-points": "6",
      "6-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Execute a pilot independent audit of a low-risk, Tier 2 vendor to validate the process.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Maintain a trusted register of local, approved auditors and penetration testing firms.</p>",
      "7": "Ability to Invoke Sovereign Inspections",
      "7-summary": "Contractual and legal rights to perform on-demand, deep technical inspections of provider platforms.",
      "7-tier": "Advanced",
      "7-points": "7",
      "7-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Establish a legal playbook and funding mechanism to invoke and execute an on-demand vendor inspection.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Conduct an internal readiness exercise to define the technical scope of a possible inspection.</p>",
      "8": "Continuous Security Control Validation",
      "8-summary": "Automated, ongoing testing and validation of the effectiveness of security and sovereign controls.",
      "8-tier": "Advanced",
      "8-points": "8",
      "8-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Implement a Security Validation Program that continuously tests control effectiveness, not just compliance.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Deploy a Breach and Attack Simulation (BAS) platform to automate sovereign control testing.</p>"
    },  
    "Domain-5" : {
      "overview" : "This domain focuses on the strategic adoption and engagement with Open Source Software (OSS) as a tool for mitigating vendor dependency and strengthening technical control. Using OSS is a powerful way to eliminate proprietary lock-in, but maturity requires more than just consumption. A mature organization actively assesses the stability and health of its OSS dependencies, develops internal expertise to audit and modify the code, and strategically contributes to critical projects. This engagement ensures the organization has the necessary influence and a viable 'forking' contingency plan should a vital OSS project become unstable or commercially compromised.",
      "qnum" : "5",
      "title" : "Open Source",
      "1": "OSS Policy and Usage Guidelines",
      "1-summary": "Established rules for the procurement, use, and license compliance of Open Source Software.",
      "1-tier": "Foundation",
      "1-points": "1",
      "1-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Draft a policy defining acceptable licenses (e.g., MIT, Apache 2.0) and prohibited OSS usage.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Implement a Static Application Security Testing (SAST) tool that flags license violations.</p>",
      "2": "Internal OSS Skills and Expertise",
      "2-summary": "Basic internal training and awareness of Open Source technologies.",
      "2-tier": "Foundation",
      "2-points": "2",
      "2-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Conduct an internal survey to identify existing staff expertise in core OSS technologies (e.g., Linux, Kubernetes).</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Allocate a percentage of engineering time to OSS training/self-study related to critical dependencies.</p>",
      "3": "Source Code Escrow Arrangements",
      "3-summary": "Legal agreements to hold critical proprietary source code in escrow.",
      "3-tier": "Foundation",
      "3-points": "3",
      "3-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Identify all COTS (Commercial Off-The-Shelf) software where source code escrow is required.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Document the process for triggering and utilizing the escrowed code in case of vendor failure.</p>",
      "4": "Dependency Risk Assessment",
      "4-summary": "Formal process for analysing the security and stability risks of third-party OSS libraries used.",
      "4-tier": "Strategic",
      "4-points": "4",
      "4-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Mandate a quarterly review of all critical OSS dependencies for security vulnerabilities and project health.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Implement a Software Composition Analysis (SCA) tool to manage the SBOM for all applications.</p>",
      "5": "Forking Strategy for Critical OSS",
      "5-summary": "A prepared plan to take over and maintain critical OSS dependencies if the original project fails.",
      "5-tier": "Strategic",
      "5-points": "5",
      "5-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Identify the Top 3 most critical OSS components and assign an internal team to develop a contingency plan (fork).</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Perform a non-production 'shadow fork' of one critical component to validate the ability to sustain it.</p>",
      "6": "Contribution to Strategic OSS Projects",
      "6-summary": "Active, strategic contribution of code and resources to OSS projects critical to the organisation.",
      "6-tier": "Advanced",
      "6-points": "6",
      "6-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Allocate an annual budget and specific developer time for direct contributions to key OSS projects.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Establish a dedicated internal team whose performance is measured by external OSS contributions.</p>",
      "7": "Active OSS Community Engagement",
      "7-summary": "Deep involvement in key Open Source communities to influence development direction.",
      "7-tier": "Advanced",
      "7-points": "7",
      "7-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Designate technical leaders to actively participate in the governance or steering committees of critical OSS projects.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Host or sponsor local meetups or conferences related to strategic open-source technologies.</p>",
      "8": "Ability to Influence OSS Roadmaps",
      "8-summary": "Strategic engagement with Open Source foundations to steer future software development.",
      "8-tier": "Advanced",
      "8-points": "8",
      "8-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Develop a formal lobbying strategy to align critical OSS project roadmaps with organizational sovereignty needs.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Submit and successfully merge a large-scale pull request that adds a sovereignty-related feature to a critical project.</p>"
        },
    "Domain-6" : {
      "qnum" : "6",
      "overview" : "Executive Oversight assesses the formal governance, sponsorship, and strategic integration of Digital Sovereignty principles across the entire organization. This ensures that sovereignty is not just an IT concern, but a core business strategy driven from the top. Maturity is measured by the establishment of clear policies, dedicated budget allocation, and the creation of a formal Sovereignty Governance Board with decision-making authority. High maturity indicates that sovereignty metrics are defined as Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) and regularly reported to the executive board, fostering a pervasive organizational culture of sovereign awareness.",
      "title" : "Executive Oversight",
      "1": "Designated Executive Sponsor ",
      "1-summary": "A senior executive has been officially assigned responsibility for Digital Sovereignty.",
      "1-tier": "Foundation",
      "1-points": "1",
      "1-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Formally appoint a C-level executive (e.g., COO, CIO) to champion Digital Sovereignty. Define the Terms of Reference (ToR) and authority of the Executive Sponsor.</p>",
      "2": "Defined Digital Sovereignty Policy",
      "2-summary": "A written policy outlining the organisation's stance and requirements on digital sovereignty.",
      "2-tier": "Foundation",
      "2-points": "2",
      "2-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Create a high-level, formal policy signed by the CEO that defines the organization's sovereignty stance. Distribute the policy to all employees and key external partners.</p>",
      "3": "Budget Allocation for Sovereignty Initiatives",
      "3-summary": "Dedicated financial resources are allocated to implement sovereignty controls and programs.",
      "3-tier": "Foundation",
      "3-points": "3",
      "3-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Include a specific, line-item budget request for digital sovereignty controls in the next budget cycle. Tie budget spend to the completion of Foundation-level capabilities.  </p>",
      "4": "Integration into Organisational Strategy",
      "4-summary": "Digital Sovereignty objectives are formally included in the overall business and technology strategy.",
      "4-tier": "Strategic",
      "4-points": "4",
      "4-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Ensure Digital Sovereignty is a key section in the next 3-year IT and Business Strategy document.</p>",
      "5": "Regular Reporting to the Board",
      "5-summary": "Consistent, periodic reporting of digital sovereignty risks and status to the executive board.",
      "5-tier": "Strategic",
      "5-points": "5",
      "5-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Establish a quarterly Dashboard presentation for the Board on sovereignty risk and progress. Define 'Red/Amber/Green' risk tolerances for the Board to approve.</p>",
      "6": "Sovereignty Culture and Awareness Program",
      "6-summary": "Ongoing training and communication ensure all staff understand their role in maintaining DS.",
      "6-tier": "Strategic",
      "6-points": "6",
      "6-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Launch an annual mandatory training module on Digital Sovereignty and employee responsibilities. Publish an internal case study detailing how a sovereignty control prevented a real-world risk.</p>",
      "7": "Dedicated Sovereignty Governance Board",
      "7-summary": "A formal, cross-functional committee regularly monitors and directs sovereignty initiatives.",
      "7-tier": "Advanced",
      "7-points": "7",
      "7-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Establish a cross-functional steering committee (Legal, IT, Procurement, Executive Sponsor) that meets monthly. Give the board the authority to veto major procurement decisions based on sovereignty risk.</p>",
      "8": "Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) Defined",
      "8-summary": "Specific, measurable metrics are used to track and assess the success of sovereignty goals.",
      "8-tier": "Advanced",
      "8-points": "8",
      "8-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Define and track quantitative KPIs related to sovereignty (e.g., % of data localized, # of high-risk vendors retired). Publish the annual sovereignty progress report (internal or external).</p>"
        },
    "Domain-7" : {
      "overview" : "This technically focused domain scrutinizes the specific control and configuration mechanisms within public and private cloud environments. As modern organizations rely heavily on cloud infrastructure, this domain ensures the organization is sovereign within its rented space. It measures the ability to enforce strict local residency for resources and network traffic, manage encryption keys independently, and audit the provider's control plane activity. Achieving Advanced maturity means the organization has a fully tested multi-cloud exit strategy, ensuring no single cloud vendor can hold critical workloads hostage.",
      "qnum" : "7",
      "title" : "Managed Services",
      "1": "Region and Zoning Control",
      "1-summary": "The ability to select and enforce specific sovereign physical regions and availability zones for all resources.",
      "1-tier": "Foundation",
      "1-points": "1",
      "1-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Enforce a 'No Auto-Deployment' policy to ensure all services are explicitly deployed to approved regions.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Configure Cloud Policy Engines (e.g., Azure Policy, AWS SCPs) to deny resource creation outside sovereign zones.</p>",
      "2": "Sovereign Image and Container Registry",
      "2-summary": "Use of internal or locally hosted repositories for all virtual machine images and application containers.",
      "2-tier": "Foundation",
      "2-points": "2",
      "2-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Mandate that all production container images are pulled from the internal registry only.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Deploy and secure a private container registry (e.g., Harbor, self-hosted Docker Registry) within the sovereign environment.</p>",
      "3": "Resource Dependency Mapping",
      "3-summary": "A clear inventory and mapping of all cloud resources and their specific dependencies on vendor-managed services.",
      "3-tier": "Foundation",
      "3-points": "3",
      "3-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Conduct a monthly review to identify any new services deployed that rely on vendor-managed global services.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Use Cloud Inventory Tools to generate a complete list of managed and unmanaged services in use.</p>",
      "4": "Hyperscaler Data Access Vetting",
      "4-summary": "Formal review and control over the specific access rights of the Cloud Provider's technical staff to your environment.",
      "4-tier": "Strategic",
      "4-points": "4",
      "4-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Contractually require strict auditing and prior approval for any hyperscaler personnel accessing the environment.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Utilize VPC Service Controls or equivalent network segmentation to block unnecessary communication with vendor management tools.</p>",
      "5": "Network Egress/Ingress Path Control",
      "5-summary": "Ownership and enforcement of all network routing, ensuring data enters and leaves via sovereign gateways.",
      "5-tier": "Strategic",
      "5-points": "5",
      "5-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Define a policy that requires all internet traffic to traverse a sovereign network firewall/gateway.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Implement dedicated interconnects/express routes that land in a local, controlled data center.</p>",
      "6": "Configuration-as-Code Ownership",
      "6-summary": "Exclusive control and independent hosting of all Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) templates and configuration files.",
      "6-tier": "Strategic",
      "6-points": "6",
      "6-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Mandate that all cloud infrastructure deployment is done via code stored in the internal Git repository.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Use an internal CI/CD pipeline to enforce that cloud configuration can only be deployed from a trusted environment.</p>",
      "7": "Control Plane Audit and Integrity",
      "7-summary": "Continuous, independent monitoring and verification of the cloud provider's underlying management plane integrity.",
      "7-tier": "Advanced",
      "7-points": "7",
      "7-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Establish an independent audit function to monitor managed service health and status pages for external influence.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Integrate Cloud Audit Logs (e.g., CloudTrail, Azure Activity Log) into an on-premises SIEM for independent retention.</p>",
      "8": "Multi-Cloud Exit Strategy Testing",
      "8-summary": "Regularly tested, automated mechanisms to quickly migrate critical workloads to a different cloud or on-premises environment.",
      "8-tier": "Advanced",
      "8-points": "8",
      "8-recommendation": "<h2 class='why-what'>Executive </h2><p>Budget and schedule a full annual test of workload migration to a secondary, non-primary cloud provider.</p><h2 class='why-what'>Technical</h2><p>Develop and maintain mirror Infrastructure-as-Code templates compatible with a secondary cloud provider for all critical assets.</p>"
        }
    } 
